<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William of Gellone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 755–812</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint William of Gellone, William of Toulouse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William of Gellone was a Frankish noble and kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> who became count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> around 790. He defended the southern frontier against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, fighting at the Orbieu in 793 and taking part in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801. In 804 he founded the monastery of Gellone, now Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert, where he died as a monk; he was later venerated as a saint and celebrated in the chansons de geste.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-of-gellone.jpg" alt="William of Gellone" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guercino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guercino_Guglielmo_d%27Aquitania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
